+\begin{theorem}\leanok
+[Normalized local Doob-Meyer decomposition]\label{thm:local_doobMeyer_normalized}
+  \uses{thm:local_doobMeyer, lem:Martingale.add_const_fun, lem:runningSup_norm_sub_const_le}
+  \lean{ProbabilityTheory.IsLocalSubmartingale.doob_meyer_normalized}
+Let \(X\) be a càdlàg local submartingale.  Then there exist processes
+\(M\) and \(A\) such that \(X=M+A\), \(M\) is a càdlàg local martingale,
+\(A\) is strongly progressive, càdlàg, locally integrable, nondecreasing,
+and \(A_0=0\).
+\end{theorem}
+
+\begin{proof}
+
+\leanok
+Apply the local Doob--Meyer decomposition to get \(X=M+A\), where \(M\)
+is a càdlàg local martingale and \(A\) is strongly progressive, càdlàg,
+locally integrable, and nondecreasing.  Define the time-constant process
+\(C_t(\omega)=A_0(\omega)\), then set
+\[
+  \widetilde A_t=A_t-C_t,
+  \qquad
+  \widetilde M_t=M_t+C_t .
+\]
+Then \(X=\widetilde M+\widetilde A\) by pointwise algebra and
+\(\widetilde A_0=0\).  Subtracting the same random variable from every
+time does not change monotonicity of the paths, and subtracting a
+time-constant càdlàg path from a càdlàg path is again càdlàg.
+
+It remains to justify two closure facts.  First, \(\widetilde M=M+C\) is a
+local martingale.  Localize simultaneously by the localizing sequence for
+\(M\) and by a localizing sequence witnessing the locally integrable
+running supremum of \(A\).  After this localization, the stopped version of
+\(M+C\) is the sum of the stopped version of \(M\), which is a martingale,
+and the time-constant process with value
+\[
+  \mathbf 1_{\{0<\tau_n\}} A_0 .
+\]
+This random variable is \(\mathcal F_0\)-measurable because \(A\) is
+strongly progressive, and its integrability follows from the localized
+running supremum of \(A\) at time \(0\).  Hence the stopped time-constant
+process is a martingale by Lemma~\ref{lem:Martingale.add_const_fun}, and
+the sum is a martingale.
+
+Second, \(\widetilde A=A-C\) has locally integrable running supremum.  Use
+the same localizing sequence as for \(A\).  For each stopped process, the
+paths of \(\widetilde A\) start from \(0\) and are nonnegative and
+nondecreasing.  Therefore the running supremum of
+\(|\widetilde A|\) up to time \(t\) is bounded by the localized running
+supremum of \(|A|\) plus the localized value \(|A_0|\); the pointwise
+estimate is Lemma~\ref{lem:runningSup_norm_sub_const_le}.  The first term
+is integrable by the local integrable-sup witness for \(A\), and the second
+is integrable because it is bounded by the same stopped running supremum at
+time \(0\).  The strong measurability of this running-supremum process is
+transported through the same subtraction by the time-constant initial value.
+Together with strong progressivity of \(A-C\), these two closure facts give
+the normalized decomposition.
+\end{proof}
